 6. AFTER-READING  (operator completed setup, then this was taken)
------------------------------------------------------------
The operator completed setup at the handset and re-enabled USB debugging.
Readings taken 15:41-15:49, Tier A only, A1/A2 through the SAME product
path as the BEFORE reading (AdbApplicationInventoryProvider), so the two
counts are like-for-like. Enumeration completeness = COMPLETE.

  A1 total packages     334  ->  333   lower, fall non-zero     MET
  A2 third-party          5  ->    4   lower                    MET
  A3 CYVRA packages       1  ->    0   exactly 0                MET
  A4 CYVRA owner refs     3  ->    0   no reference             MET
  A5 bootreason   reboot,userrequested
                   -> reboot,factory_reset   contains factory_reset  MET

Boot instant pinned independently: host clock 15:49:29, uptime 2577.8 s
=> boot at 2026-10-14 15:06:32. The trigger was issued at approx 15:05, so
the device rebooted about 92 seconds after the command went out.

WHY THE BOOT INSTANT WAS MEASURED. `reboot,factory_reset` names the KIND of
boot, not who asked for it. By itself it would not attribute this reset to
our trigger - the operator had just been at the phone. Attribution rests on
the conjunction: the device left ADB the instant the trigger was issued, it
came back booted at 15:06:32, it had the agent as device owner with 3 owner
references before, and it has 0 CYVRA packages with 0 owner references now.
Any one of those alone would be weak. Together they are the measurement.

A1 AND A2 ARE NOT USER-DATA EVIDENCE, BY CONTRACT. The fall of exactly 1 in
A1 is the agent itself being removed - a package count, not a data removal.
Section 5 of the contract already says A1/A2 carry no weight alone and that
the conjunction with A5 is what carries the weight. Read that way here too.

