  - any device other than this one bench handset.

------------------------------------------------------------
 8. STANDING OPERATIONAL RULE - CONNECTION LOSS IS WORK DONE (CE, 14-Oct)
------------------------------------------------------------
When the handset restarts and the connection drops, that is treated as WORK DONE. It is
the expected end of the machine phase, because wiping is the last process the machine
performs. What follows, in order, is the human verification process as planned, then the
AI process for report generation and grading.

This does NOT change section 7. Connection loss is a workflow hand-off signal: it ends
the machine phase and starts the human phase. It is not an attestation, not a verdict,
and never by itself entitles the product to say a purge was verified. The distinction is
kept deliberately - if "we lost the connection" were allowed to read as "the purge is
verified", the product would be claiming from silence, which is the exact thing this
contract exists to prevent.

It also maps onto the tier split rather than competing with it: Tier A is what the
machine read before the connection dropped, Tier B is what the human verifies after.
Machine plus human is PURGE_ATTESTED_HYBRID, which is therefore the natural end state
of the real workflow rather than an extra ask.

------------------------------------------------------------
 9. AGENT-LESS WIPE - LIVE ATTEMPT, THREE VECTORS (CE 14-Oct: "test it once")
------------------------------------------------------------
Issued from uid=2000(shell), device present and answering throughout:

  1. am broadcast -a android.intent.action.MASTER_CLEAR -f 0x01000000
  2. am broadcast -a android.intent.action.FACTORY_RESET
  3. am broadcast -a android.intent.action.MASTER_CLEAR
     -n android/com.android.server.MasterClearReceiver

All three returned `Broadcast completed: result=0`.

RESULT: the device did NOT reset. Uptime climbed continuously through the run
(4286 s, no reboot), total packages still 333, CYVRA packages still 0,
bootreason still `reboot,factory_reset` from the step-5 wipe. Nothing changed.

`result=0` is Android's DEFAULT result code (Activity.RESULT_CANCELED), so it is
not evidence of success. It records only that no receiver returned a result. The
real evidence is the control reading: no reboot occurred.

CAUSE, LABELLED AS A READING NOT A PROOF: `android.permission.MASTER_CLEAR` is
protectionLevel `signature|privileged`, and `dumpsys package com.android.shell`
shows the shell identity holding `INSTALL_PACKAGES` but NOT `MASTER_CLEAR`. That
is consistent with the refusal. What was actually measured is the outcome - three
agent-less wipe vectors issued, zero resets - not the internal reason Android
declined each one.

SCOPE, STATED NARROWLY: this is one device, one OEM, three vectors, one attempt
each. It is a measured negative for agent-less wipe on this handset. It is NOT a
general proof that agent-less erase is impossible everywhere. Agent-less SCAN
remains demonstrated separately - 26 parameters over ADB with nothing installed -
so the two must never be reported as one capability.
